The one idea behind all axes
----------------------------
An XPath always starts from a context node, the element you’re currently standing on. An axis is the 
direction you walk from that node.

The 11 axes in four groups

Group	         Axes	                                 |    Mental picture

Up	             parent, ancestor, ancestor-or-self 	 |   Climb toward html
Down	         child, descendant, descendant-or-self	 |   Dig into the subtree
Sideways	     preceding-sibling, following-sibling	 |   Look at siblings under the same parent
Document order	 preceding, following	                 |   Everything earlier or later in the page source

self is just the node you’re standing on.


The confusing pairs
--------------------

These are the pairs that trip most people up. Try each in the diagram with li[2] as the context(starting element):

parent vs ancestor: parent is exactly one step up (ul). ancestor is the whole chain up (ul, main, body, html).
child vs descendant: child is one level down (span, a). descendant is every depth. Here they match because li[2] has no grandchildren, so click ul and compare.
following-sibling vs following: siblings share the same parent, so you get only li[3]. following is everything later in the document, so you get li[3], p, and footer. It skips descendants of the context node, which is why span and a are not included.
preceding vs preceding-sibling: the same pattern in reverse. preceding skips ancestors, so ul, main, body, and html never appear.


My doubt came :following-sibling vs following: this is not very clear. Which element we are 
starting from here and explain in detail and easy language

Answer:
following-sibling: stay in the same family
-------------------------------------------

A sibling is an element with the same parent. The parent of li[2] is the ul, so its siblings are the other items inside that ul: li[1] and li[3].

following-sibling means “the siblings that come after me”, so the result is only li[3].

The other elements fail the “same parent” test:

p has a different parent (main), so it is not a sibling.
footer has a different parent (body).
span and a are inside li[2], so they are children, not siblings.


following: everything after me in the page
------------------------------------------
following ignores families. It asks: “If I finish reading li[2] (I’ve passed its closing </li>), what 
elements come next in the page source?” The answer is li[3], then p, then footer. 
They live in different parents, but they all come later.

It doesn’t include <span> and <a> because they sit inside li[2], before its closing tag.
You haven’t finished li[2] when you read them, so they don’t count as “after”.


Side by side
------------
Question	                  following-sibling::*	            following::*
---------
Same parent required?	      Yes                         	    No
Selects from li[2]	        li[3]	                            li[3], p, footer
Includes p?	                No	                              Yes
Includes span, a?	          No                              	No (they’re inside li[2])

"following-sibling" is always a subset of "following". Everything it finds, following finds too, plus more.